# Firewall Policy

The design follows a least-privilege model.

ATTACK_NET was not granted unrestricted access to CORP-LAN.

Instead, a narrowly scoped test rule was created.

## Controlled ICMP Rule

```text
Action:       PASS
Interface:    ATTACK_NET
Protocol:     ICMP
ICMP Type:    Echo Request

Source:
10.10.20.10
KALI01

Destination:
10.10.10.102
WIN11-02

Logging:
Enabled
```

Conceptually:

```text
KALI01
10.10.20.10
     |
     | ICMP Echo Request
     v
+----------+
|   FW01   |
| pfSense  |
+----------+
     |
     | Explicit PASS
     v
WIN11-02
10.10.10.102
```

The rule permits the required test traffic without granting the entire ATTACK_NET unrestricted access to CORP-LAN.

---

# Windows Host Firewall

Network firewall permission alone was not sufficient.

WIN11-02 also required a narrowly scoped Windows Defender Firewall rule allowing ICMP from KALI01.

The rule was created with:

```powershell
New-NetFirewallRule `
  -DisplayName "LAB - Allow ICMP from KALI01" `
  -Direction Inbound `
  -Protocol ICMPv4 `
  -IcmpType 8 `
  -RemoteAddress 10.10.20.10 `
  -Action Allow `
  -Profile Any
```

This created defense-in-depth:

```text
KALI01
   |
   | ICMP
   v
pfSense Firewall
   |
   | Explicit permission
   v
Windows Firewall
   |
   | Explicit permission from KALI01
   v
WIN11-02
```

Both network and endpoint policy therefore control access.

