# Troubleshooting Case Study

## Problem

KALI01 could not initially ping WIN11-02 despite an explicit pfSense rule permitting the traffic.

```text
KALI01
10.10.20.10
     |
     | ICMP
     v
FW01
     |
     ?
     v
WIN11-02
10.10.10.102
```

The initial result was:

```text
4 packets transmitted
0 received
100% packet loss
```

---

## Step 1 — Validate FW01 to WIN11-02

FW01 successfully pinged:

```text
10.10.10.102
```

Result:

```text
4 packets transmitted
4 packets received
0% packet loss
```

This proved the CORP-LAN path between FW01 and WIN11-02 was functional.

---

## Step 2 — Validate pfSense Rule

The active ruleset was inspected:

```sh
pfctl -sr
```

The expected rule was present:

```text
KALI01 10.10.20.10
        |
        | ICMP
        v
WIN11-02 10.10.10.102
```

---

## Step 3 — Inspect Rule Counters

Using:

```sh
pfctl -vvsr
```

packet counters showed traffic matching the rule.

This proved KALI01 traffic was reaching FW01 and matching the correct firewall policy.

---

## Step 4 — Capture Traffic

Traffic leaving FW01 toward CORP-LAN was observed using:

```sh
tcpdump -ni em1 icmp and host 10.10.10.102
```

Echo requests were visible.

Echo replies were not.

This significantly narrowed the troubleshooting scope.

---

## Step 5 — Investigate Endpoint Policy

Since:

```text
KALI01 -> FW01             PASS
FW01 firewall rule         PASS
FW01 -> CORP-LAN           PASS
FW01 -> WIN11-02           REACHABLE
```

attention shifted to WIN11-02.

A dedicated Windows Firewall rule was created permitting ICMPv4 Echo Requests only from:

```text
10.10.20.10
```

---

## Resolution

After applying the scoped Windows Firewall rule:

```bash
ping -c 4 10.10.10.102
```

succeeded from KALI01.

Final path:

```text
KALI01
10.10.20.10
     |
     | ICMP
     v
FW01 ATTACK_NET
     |
     | pfSense PASS
     v
FW01 CORP-LAN
     |
     v
WIN11-02 Windows Firewall
     |
     | Allow from 10.10.20.10
     v
WIN11-02
10.10.10.102

SUCCESS
```

---

# Segmentation Validation

The final configuration demonstrated both permitted and restricted traffic.

The security model is:

```text
ATTACK_NET
10.10.20.0/24
      |
      | Default restricted access
      v
    FW01
      |
      +---- Explicit exceptions only
      |
      v
CORP-LAN
10.10.10.0/24
```

This prevents placement of a system on ATTACK_NET from automatically granting access to the corporate environment.

